  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Alder Bracket (Mensularia radiata) is a wood-decay fungus in the Hymenochaetaceae family, characterized by its distinctive shelf-like fruiting bodies. It primarily colonizes alder trees and other hardwoods in moist forest environments throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Radial ridges",
      "description": "The underside displays prominent radiating ridges that extend from a central point, resembling spokes on a wheel",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Bracket shape",
      "description": "Forms hoof-shaped or semicircular shelves that project from the tree trunk without a stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Rusty coloration",
      "description": "Upper surface is typically rusty-brown to dark brown with a cinnamon-colored underside",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Mensularia radiata grows on dead or dying alder, birch, and other hardwoods in moist, deciduous forests and riparian zones throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus name Mensularia refers to its table-like or shelf appearance, while radiata describes the distinctive radiating ridges that make it one of the most visually recognizable bracket fungi in the region.",
